  "title": "How to Solve Linear Equations",
  "content": "<p>A <strong>linear equation</strong> has the unknown raised to the power of 1 (no \\(x^2\\) or higher). Solving means finding the value of the unknown that makes the equation true.</p><p><strong>Key principle:</strong> Whatever you do to one side, do the same to the other. Use inverse operations to isolate the unknown.</p><p><strong>Equations with brackets:</strong> Expand brackets first, then solve. Alternatively, you can sometimes divide both sides by the bracket coefficient first.</p><p><strong>Unknowns on both sides:</strong> Collect all terms containing the unknown on one side and constants on the other, then solve.</p><p><strong>Equations with fractions:</strong> Multiply every term by the lowest common denominator to clear fractions, then solve as normal.</p>",
  "example": "<p><strong>Solve</strong> \\(3(2x - 1) = 4x + 9\\)</p><p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Expand: \\(6x - 3 = 4x + 9\\)</p><p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Subtract \\(4x\\): \\(2x - 3 = 9\\)</p><p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Add 3: \\(2x = 12\\)</p><p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Divide by 2: \\(x = 6\\)</p>"
 },
